d with  every opportunity to observe their  material at close range often intimately  and is the intimate personal penetrating  perceptive touch indeed that is equipped  to furnish a chapter that would be  missing where the camera excludes  we relied upon this manifest  preponderantly on the human side our  digest associate editor mod Riley simile  stated that the works on display make a  thrilling story one more real than words  reproductions and news photographs Riley  further singled out Peter herds  depiction of a tail gunner and Tom lees  paintings of the Hornet as especially  powerful a reviewer for The New Yorker  likewise lauded life for recording the  war and paint and wrote quote it's a  good thing for all of us that life  undertook the job for his things have  turned out if it hadn't done so we would  now have practically no artistic record  of the fighting so far in contrast  Jerome elk was writing for the nation  labeled the artist feeble recorders and  critique their art on display at the met  as conveying little all too little of  wars calamity heroism humor depth and  grief and further criticized the  institutions who hosted the exhibition  for displaying such weak examples like  some critics some artists also found  life's war are to be superficial  unemotional and uncritical in 1944 a  group of leftist artists each of whom  for different reasons had not gone to  the Front's protested life's traveling  exhibition of war art which was  currently on display at the Met by  mounting a show of their own organised  by artist Fernando Puma at his gallery  on west 57th Street in New York the  exhibition entitled we challenge war art  argued that life could not be left  unchallenged Puma and his fellow artists  in the exhibition including max where  George gross Victor fall John growth  William proper and Seymour Lipton thus  offered their alternative view of the  war as soon as life got wind of the  exhibition they published a spread on  Puma show life repo