are  individually capable of and the most  integrated picture of war and all its  phases any subject is in order if as  artists you feel it is part of war  battle scenes and front lines battle  landscapes and the wounded the dying and  the dead prisoners of war field  hospitals and base hospitals wrecked  habitations and bombing scenes character  sketches of our own troops  of prisoners of the natives of the  country you visit the nobility courage  cowardice cruelty boredom of war omit  nothing duplicate to your heart's  content you may be guided by blakes  mysticism by Goya cynicism and sad  savagery or by dela Paz romanticism by  domi ace humanity and tenderness or  better still follow your own inevitable  star life's war artist sought to capture  all aspects of war and they rendered  their varied experiences in a wide  variety of styles and media from quick  spent pencil sketches and delicate  watercolors to expressive and even  cubist style oil paintings such as these  by reuben Kadesh bernard perlin covered  events in greece in a series of  narrative figurative paintings that life  later published in its februari 26 1945  issue life assigned artist Miller sheets  to report on India where he chose to  paint the starving poor and scenes of  suffering civilians and stark expression  acidic images life war artist David  Frieden Thal accompanied a Marine unit  on a night attack on Japanese occupied  island in the South Pacific that  resulted in a powerful series of  paintings later that year he went to  Yugoslavia to cover Tito's partisans and  he was the first to depict the Russian  army in Yugoslavia at a time when  neither allied reporters nor  photographers were allowed access to the  Russian front of all of the images  produced by life war artists one of the  most common images was that of  architectural destruction resulting from  more and for more on these images there  were over 500 of these kind of images  that circulated in life during the war  I'm see my recent essay and Joanne  Mancini and Keith Bresnan president's  volume on Life magazine and  architectural destruction while  stationed in Britain for example the  American artist Byron Thomas regularly  painted ruins both secular and sacred in  a variety of styles including this  painting of Coventry Cathedral and ruins  with its empty tracery set against a  dusky sky and scraggly Crosslake tree  form at the center it is both a haunting  depiction of present-day destruction and  reminiscent of the romantic landscapes  of Caspar David Friedrich Thomas also  painted and surrealist rendering of  ghosts like streets of East End London  as well as ruined residences replete  with Victory Gardens such images of  ruined buildings with Gardens  additionally connected to the garden  ruined tradition often found in  picturesque landscapes whether painted  or a part of an English countryside  life's artists were not the only ones to  link the destroyed buildings resulting  from World War two to garden ruins on  August fifteenth nineteen forty four in  response to proposals to either rebuild  or restored the damaged cathedrals of  England sir kenneth clark who is also  the chair of the british war artist  advisory committee john maynard keynes  TS Eliot and other prominent  intellectuals and art historians wrote a  letter to the London Times which called  to preserve the bomb damage churches in  their ruined condition as permanent  memorials of the war in 1945 they  published their proposal as a pamphlet  entitled bomb churches as war memorials  the book included sketches of the ruined  buildings as they would look if allowed  to both naturally and artificially grow  and to garden ruins a proposal which to  paraphrase historian Christopher  Woodward could only have originated in  England life's war artist stationed in  England not only painted romantic images  of wartime ruins but also unlike what  life's war artist stationed elsewhere in  the world spent much time depicting the  daily experiences of the British people  during the war as well as scenes of  American soldiers interacting with  British civilians ranging from images of  East End children entertaining  themselves among broken walls and bomb  shelters to a meditative scene of an  American soldier and a Scottish  companion gazing across the river tay  and perth two paintings of an  international mix of soldiers and  civilians listening to a nine o'clock  news broadcast in scotland even when  portraying the arrival of american  planes in england's airfields in advance  of d-day as shown here in ogden place  nurs painting or in byron Thomas's  painting of their arrival of an American  transport ship in England one gets the  sense of a shared cause and experience  of war that the British and American  people troops and artists are indeed in  the same boat in fact the caption  beneath this picture describes how quote  this little British cutter  we'll steer the American ship safely in  indeed many of the captions and titles  that accompanied images of the British  front often had a tone of solidarity and  empathy of working together in the fight  as well as the emotional toll that the  war took such as in this headline that  said British are battered but bored by  the long war which accompanied a  painting by the American artist Aaron  bow rod life hired artists to paint the  war to lend variety and also credibility  to its pictorial reporting in an  internal memo to fellow publisher Andrew  high school Roy Larson wrote quote  strangely enough despite the fantastic  success of life and other picture  magazines news photography is not a  respectable profession and the  photograph is not considered to be a  complete and accurate medium for the  reproduction of news and general  information indeed as life articulated  in early nineteen forty-three  advertisement the objective was quote to  get a series of pictures that will make  life readers feel they have actually I  witnessed the great events taking place  at the front and for them that could not  be accomplished through the photograph  alone indeed although life employed some  of the world's best photographers its  editors were well aware of the technical  limitations of the camera even though by  the 1920s and 30s more compact and  portable cameras had been developed with  faster film more powerful lenses and  accelerated shutter speeds the camera  could not capture anything everything  moreover both before and during World  War two color photography was not  technically advanced enough to shoot all  aspects of war and life clearly wanted  to supplement its black-and-white  photographic coverage with color images  as Daniel longwa wrote in a memo when  the war broke out those of us who got  around the country at various training  camps realized color had to be used I  took a trip out to visit the Marines at  Elliot field in San Diego and the Army  at Fort Sam Houston and one of the  things I remember about the war artists  was that Eastman had not yet developed  the fast color film and the color film  procedure was too slow to report the war  in action thus the war artist could  record with their pencil brush or pen  what the photographer cannot capture  with a camera due to both technical and  environmental limitations as well as  security  and military restrictions artists could  also add in details that they would not  have been able to photograph at the time  because of safety issues or physical or  visual obstructions paintings unlike  photographs could also present the  events of war in a continuous narrative  within one picture whereas a photograph  reproduces the appearance of one  instance from one point of view a  painter could construct his image to  show a progression of events within a  single scene or to even show multiple  viewpoints simultaneously indeed many of  life's war artists had prior  professional training and knew well the  pictorial conventions for both history  and narrative painting and utilize them  as they render depictions of battles or  other events of war as life war artist  Fletcher Martin argued his paintings  were more personal and perhaps at least  in terms of the subjective emotional  experience of war even more accurate  than the camera he wrote the painter can  make a more personal statement than the  camera his record can be stronger than  the cameras because he can eliminate  unnecessary detail and concentrate on  the significant some of my work that  digressed frankly from actuality met the  approval of the fighting men themselves  they responded to it and said I'd got  the feel of the action that was the acid  test furthermore unlike the photograph  which had to be made on the spot and the  serves as an indexical record of the  photographer having been there a  painting could variously be painted on  site be made after the fact or from  memory who could even conjecture what  might happen this allowed artists to  capture events they may have been  otherwise impossible and many of the war  artists actually kept lengthy journals  and sketchbooks to record their thoughts  so that they could paint from memory  when they returned to their studios in  some cases life even reproduced some of  the artist sketch books such as this  these of Erin bow rod and Byron Thomas  who in 1944 six days after the Normandy  invasion hitchhiked their way from  England across France and arrived at  Omaha Beach life reprinted their sketch  book in its october 9th 1944 issue and  come when compared with Robert Kappas  powerful photographs of d-day that were  published in life in 1945 they  demonstrate how different media offered  varied views of the war for life the  combination of paintings drawings and  photo  graphs and text could better tell the  whole story as Daniel long will later  recollected although we covered the war  with our camera better than any other  Illustrated magazine had ever covered  the war it was our painters who added  the extra ten percent that made the show  truly notable life not only reproduced  many of these paintings and its weekly  pages they also celebrated them in a  series of traveling exhibitions in 1943  life's war art exhibition opened at the  National Gallery of Art in Washington  before traveling to the metropolitan  museum of art were over 26,000 people  attended the show the exhibition  displayed 122 paintings and watercolors  prints and drawings that were made in  the United States battle zones by nine  of life's war artists Frances Hannah  Taylor the director of the Metropolitan  Museum of Art in his introduction to the  catalog contextualized life's war art  within the history of art comparing them  to such iconic history paintings as  Benjamin West death of General Wolfe and  Lloyds as Washington Crossing the  Delaware Taylor then went on to  emphasize that what distinguished life's  War art from earlier examples was that  artists like west &amp; Lloyd's of quote  were seldom eyewitnesses to the scenes  they depicted instead their works were  painted out of the fragments of  recollection hear the word of an  eyewitness there and a liberal dash of  romantic imagination from somewhere else  in contrast Taylor argued life's artists  reporters observed history in the raw an  aspect which struck Taylor as having  high significance and after its showing  at the Met it went on a nationwide tour  the show was incredibly popular with the  public in part due to life's extensive  publication of many of these pictures  and its pages throughout the war but the  art received mixed reviews from the  critics Edward aldenjewell writing for  The New York Times was initially  skeptical and remarked that at first he  doubted that painters could compete with  the camera but after seeing the  exhibition his opinion was tellingly  reshaped and he wrote the exhibition as  a whole is impressive it contains much  fine and serious work by American  artists who have been provided with  every opportunity to observe their  material at close range often intimately  and is the intimate personal penetrating  perceptive touch indeed that is equipped  to furnish a chapter that would be  missing where the camera excludes  we relied upon this manifest  preponderantly on the human side our  digest associate editor mod Riley simile  stated that the works on display make a  thrilling story one more real than words  reproductions and news photographs Riley  further singled out Peter herds  depiction of a tail gunner and Tom lees  paintings of the Hornet as especially  powerful a reviewer for The New Yorker  likewise lauded life for recording the  war and paint and wrote quote it's a  good thing for all of us that life  undertook the job for his things have  turned out if it hadn't done so we would  now have practically no artistic record  of the fighting so far in contrast  Jerome elk was writing for the nation  labeled the artist feeble recorders and  critique their art on display at the met  as conveying little all too little of  wars calamity heroism humor depth and  grief and further criticized the  institutions who hosted the exhibition  for displaying such weak examples like  some critics some artists also found  life's war are to be superficial  unemotional and uncritical in 1944 a  group of leftist artists each of whom  for different reasons had not gone to  the Front's protested life's traveling  exhibition of war art which was  currently on display at the Met by  mounting a show of their own organised  by artist Fernando Puma at his gallery  on west 57th Street in New York the  exhibition entitled we challenge war art  argued that life could not be left  unchallenged Puma and his fellow artists  in the exhibition including max where  George gross Victor fall John growth  William proper and Seymour Lipton thus  offered their alternative view of the  war as soon as life got wind of the  exhibition they published a spread on  Puma show life reported quote mr. Puma  believes that these pictures are better  than the painting records done by life  war artist correspondence at the front  because they expressed the psychological  or philosophical reaction of artists to  war life photograph the rebel artists  with their paintings and reproduced in  color George grosses expressive and  powerful I was always present Pumas they  will not conquer and William droppers  Pearl Harbor among other works life  concluded the article by asking the  readers to judge the issue for  themselves many readers reply to the  article some thanking life for the  provocative art store  while others were appalled that those  who did not go to the front would  attempt to paint what they had not  experienced another reader argued in  favor of Pumas exhibition stating the  painting in the exhibition are much more  powerful and telling than the works of  life's war artist correspondence life  even conducted its own informal poll of  art critics art history professors  museum directors and printed the most  common response concluding quote it all  depends on the man behind the brush in  just five years life's war artist  correspondence produced over 1,000  paintings drawings and watercolours  hundreds of which were reproduced in  life both during and after the war up to  mid nineteen forty four life had printed  approximately 160 reproductions of the  670 oils watercolors and sketches that  had been done up to that time and a  total of four hundred and sixty-seven  had been reproduced in life by may nine  teen 46 the collection of war are  remained at life until nineteen sixty  when during the week of the 19th  anniversary of Pearl Harbor the 10 1050  works in the life collection of art from  World War two were presented to the  Department of Defense by editor in chief  Henry Robinson loose in a ceremony at  the Pentagon although law often left out  of histories of American Art the  thousands of paintings produced by Life  magazine artists during World War two  were not only important documents of the  war and form a significant part of the  artistic production of the 1940s in  America they were also some of the most  controversial works of the period  generating both popular and critical  praise as well as protest shaping the  American public's perception of the  varied fronts of our enemies and allies  during World War two thank  you
 

